{{/* thin_banner (DaltonSW override of headline's built-in).

     Differs from the built-in in exactly one way: the subtitle lives in a
     fixed-width, LEFT-aligned column instead of shrink-to-fit and
     right-aligned.

     Why: the built-in uses justify-content:space-between with flex:none on
     the title, so the subtitle gets whatever width the title happens to
     leave. Across a README full of these, the subtitle's left edge landed
     anywhere from x=257 to x=608 and the title->subtitle gutter swung from
     68px to 243px, so a vertical stack of banners had no column for the eye
     to lock onto. Pinning the subtitle column makes every banner share one
     vertical axis and wrap at one width.

     Second effect, deliberate: the title box is now flex:1;min-width:0
     rather than flex:none, so an over-long title (see
     Cult-of-the-Closed-Circuit) wraps to a second line at full size instead
     of needing a hand-shrunk title.size. Keep title.size uniform across
     configs; let this box do the fitting.

     The 360px subtitle column below is tuned against the 900x140 output
     these configs use, and it is a two-sided fit -- don't nudge it without
     re-rendering everything:
       - The title box gets what's left (inner width - 80 padding - 32 gap
         - 360 = 412px). At output.thin_banner.title_size 45 that is just
         wide enough to keep "I Wanna Hookshot" -- the longest title that
         still fits on one line -- unwrapped. It wraps at 46, or at 45 if
         this column grows past ~370.
       - Going much below 360 pushes the longest subtitle (aocgo, 91 chars)
         from 3 lines to 4, which is what made the old banners feel cramped.

     Vertical centering: inherited verbatim from the built-in, and load
     bearing. The row below deliberately does NOT set align-items:center on
     itself -- Ogre's flex align-items:center miscalculates the cross-axis
     offset when a row's direct children have very different intrinsic
     heights (title vs. subtitle here) and can shove the taller child down
     instead of centering it. Each direct child instead gets its own
     explicit height (.InnerHeight, px) + display:flex;align-items:center so
     it self-centers in isolation. Don't reintroduce align-items:center on
     the outer row without re-verifying at several title/body size ratios. */}}
<div style="display:flex;justify-content:center;align-items:center;width:{{.Size.Width}}px;height:{{.Size.Height}}px;box-sizing:border-box;{{borderFrameCSS .Border .Title}}">
<div style="display:flex;flex-direction:row;justify-content:space-between;width:{{.InnerWidth}}px;height:{{.InnerHeight}}px;padding:0 40px;{{canvasBackgroundCSS .Background .Title}}box-sizing:border-box;gap:32px;{{contentRadiusCSS .Border}}">

  {{/* Title (+ optional icon), left-aligned, self-centered in its own
       full-height box (see the note above). flex:1;min-width:0 lets a long
       title wrap rather than squeeze the subtitle column. */}}
  <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:{{if eq .Icon.Position "right"}}row-reverse{{else}}row{{end}};align-items:center;gap:24px;flex:1;min-width:0;height:{{.InnerHeight}}px">
    {{if notBlank .IconSource}}
    <img src="{{.IconSource}}" style="width:{{.Icon.Size}}px;height:{{.Icon.Size}}px;object-fit:contain;flex-shrink:0;" />
    {{end}}
    {{titleLineHTML .Title .Content.Title "left"}}
  </div>

  {{if notBlank .Content.Subtitle}}
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:flex-start;width:360px;flex:none;height:{{.InnerHeight}}px;font-family:body;font-size:{{.Body.Size}}px;color:{{.Body.Color}};text-align:left">{{esc .Content.Subtitle}}</div>
  {{- end}}
</div>
</div>
